1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,045
A home address gives a house
number, a street and a city.

2
00:00:04,045 --> 00:00:06,558
A cinema ticket gives a row and a seat.

3
00:00:06,558 --> 00:00:10,246
And a chemical name can give
the whole drawing of a molecule.

4
00:00:10,646 --> 00:00:11,563
Three codes.

5
00:00:11,563 --> 00:00:14,977
Read each one in parts, and
you find the exact spot.

6
00:00:15,427 --> 00:00:17,595
Aarav sees it in the college corridor.

7
00:00:17,595 --> 00:00:19,240
The lab list says propanone.

8
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:21,535
His friend calls the same bottle acetone.

9
00:00:21,885 --> 00:00:23,404
Two names, one bottle.

10
00:00:23,404 --> 00:00:27,097
And across the world, there are
millions of compounds to name.

11
00:00:27,597 --> 00:00:29,810
So chemists agreed on one rule book.

12
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:34,030
By the end, you will build a name
from a drawing, and a drawing from a

13
00:00:34,030 --> 00:00:34,930
name.

14
00:00:35,181 --> 00:00:36,952
The rule book is IUPAC.

15
00:00:36,952 --> 00:00:40,524
The International Union of
Pure and Applied Chemistry.

16
00:00:40,874 --> 00:00:45,100
An IUPAC name is built in parts,
like a train with coaches.

17
00:00:45,450 --> 00:00:47,788
The middle coach is the word root.

18
00:00:47,788 --> 00:00:51,043
It tells the number of
carbons in the main chain.

19
00:00:51,043 --> 00:00:55,660
Meth is one, eth two, prop three,
but four, pent five, hex six.

20
00:00:56,010 --> 00:00:58,252
Next comes the primary suffix.

21
00:00:58,252 --> 00:01:00,626
A n e means only single bonds.

22
00:01:00,626 --> 00:01:02,780
E n e means a double bond.

23
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:04,935
Y n e means a triple bond.

24
00:01:05,335 --> 00:01:08,918
Then the secondary suffix,
for the functional group.

25
00:01:08,918 --> 00:01:13,524
O l for an alcohol, a l for an
aldehyde, o n e for a ketone.

26
00:01:13,924 --> 00:01:17,969
And in front, the prefix, for
side groups like methyl or chloro.

27
00:01:17,969 --> 00:01:19,811
Prefix, word root, suffix.

28
00:01:19,811 --> 00:01:21,385
That is the whole name.

29
00:01:22,035 --> 00:01:25,651
Let's name a branched molecule,
on our molecule bench.

30
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:26,901
Rule one.

31
00:01:26,772 --> 00:01:28,944
Find the longest chain of carbons.

32
00:01:28,944 --> 00:01:30,636
Here it has five carbons.

33
00:01:30,636 --> 00:01:32,385
So the word root is pent.

34
00:01:32,785 --> 00:01:34,850
One carbon hangs off the side.

35
00:01:34,850 --> 00:01:37,580
ACH three side group is called methyl.

36
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:38,880
Rule two.

37
00:01:38,686 --> 00:01:41,429
Number the chain from the
end nearer the branch.

38
00:01:41,429 --> 00:01:44,129
From the left, the methyl
sits on carbon two.

39
00:01:44,529 --> 00:01:47,100
From the right, it would
sit on carbon four.

40
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:49,545
Two is lower, so we number from the left.

41
00:01:49,545 --> 00:01:51,342
This is the lowest locant rule.

42
00:01:51,342 --> 00:01:53,439
A locant is just the position number.

43
00:01:53,839 --> 00:01:55,244
Now join the parts.

44
00:01:55,244 --> 00:01:57,333
Two, dash, methyl, pentane.

45
00:01:57,333 --> 00:01:58,680
Two methyl pentane.

46
00:01:59,330 --> 00:02:00,479
More side groups?

47
00:02:00,479 --> 00:02:01,379
More rules.

48
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:04,411
If the same group
appears twice, we add di.

49
00:02:04,411 --> 00:02:05,639
Three times, tri.

50
00:02:05,639 --> 00:02:07,774
Like two, three, dimethyl butane.

51
00:02:08,174 --> 00:02:11,373
Different side groups are
written in alphabetical order.

52
00:02:11,373 --> 00:02:12,994
Ethyl comes before methyl.

53
00:02:12,994 --> 00:02:15,232
Di and tri are ignored when sorting.

54
00:02:15,632 --> 00:02:17,537
Rings get the prefix cyclo.

55
00:02:17,537 --> 00:02:20,064
A ring of six carbons is cyclohexane.

56
00:02:20,714 --> 00:02:22,808
Now molecules with a functional group.

57
00:02:22,808 --> 00:02:23,709
Link by link.

58
00:02:24,059 --> 00:02:24,959
Link one.

59
00:02:24,819 --> 00:02:26,764
Find the main functional group.

60
00:02:26,764 --> 00:02:28,144
It gives the suffix.

61
00:02:28,894 --> 00:02:29,794
Link two.

62
00:02:29,611 --> 00:02:32,674
The main chain must include
the carbon of that group.

63
00:02:33,424 --> 00:02:34,324
Link three.

64
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:37,002
Number the chain so that
group gets the lowest number.

65
00:02:37,752 --> 00:02:38,652
Link four.

66
00:02:38,443 --> 00:02:42,160
If there are many groups, the one
with higher priority wins the suffix.

67
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:43,511
Acids sit near the top.

68
00:02:43,511 --> 00:02:46,720
Lower down come aldehydes, then
ketones, alcohols and amines.

69
00:02:47,470 --> 00:02:48,370
Link five.

70
00:02:48,165 --> 00:02:49,885
Some groups are always prefixes.

71
00:02:49,885 --> 00:02:53,491
Halogens, like chloro and bromo,
and nitro, never become a suffix.

72
00:02:54,241 --> 00:02:56,522
Now compare two names side by side.

73
00:02:56,871 --> 00:02:59,667
Propane plus o l becomes propan two o l.

74
00:02:59,667 --> 00:03:03,906
The final e of propane drops,
because o l starts with a vowel.

75
00:03:04,306 --> 00:03:06,677
Propane plus o n e becomes propanone.

76
00:03:06,677 --> 00:03:10,995
For three carbons, the ketone can
only sit on carbon two, so no number

77
00:03:10,995 --> 00:03:11,895
is needed.

78
00:03:12,213 --> 00:03:13,382
Pause and think.

79
00:03:13,382 --> 00:03:16,631
A chain of four carbons
has an OH on carbon two.

80
00:03:16,631 --> 00:03:17,904
What is its name?

81
00:03:21,104 --> 00:03:22,375
Butan two o l!

82
00:03:22,375 --> 00:03:25,686
But for four carbons, an OH on carbon two.

83
00:03:25,686 --> 00:03:28,228
The e of butane drops before o l.

84
00:03:28,878 --> 00:03:29,886
Remember this.

85
00:03:29,886 --> 00:03:33,699
Longest chain, lowest numbers,
then read the parts in order.

86
00:03:34,399 --> 00:03:36,220
Now, one worked example.

87
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:37,573
Name this alcohol.

88
00:03:37,873 --> 00:03:38,773
Step one.

89
00:03:38,669 --> 00:03:41,679
The main chain must
hold the carbon with OH.

90
00:03:41,679 --> 00:03:44,271
The longest such chain has four carbons.

91
00:03:44,271 --> 00:03:45,171
But.

92
00:03:45,516 --> 00:03:46,416
Step two.

93
00:03:46,333 --> 00:03:48,136
Number from the OH end.

94
00:03:48,136 --> 00:03:50,148
The OH sits on carbon one.

95
00:03:50,898 --> 00:03:51,798
Step three.

96
00:03:51,654 --> 00:03:53,683
A methyl group sits on carbon three.

97
00:03:54,433 --> 00:03:55,333
Step four.

98
00:03:55,143 --> 00:03:56,106
Join the parts.

99
00:03:56,106 --> 00:03:57,951
Three methyl, butan, one o l.

100
00:03:58,701 --> 00:04:00,318
Three methyl butan one o l.

101
00:04:00,318 --> 00:04:02,389
The name rebuilds the drawing exactly.

102
00:04:03,039 --> 00:04:05,886
Benzene compounds have
their own short rules.

103
00:04:06,236 --> 00:04:08,361
With one group, just add it in front.

104
00:04:08,361 --> 00:04:09,261
Chlorobenzene.

105
00:04:09,163 --> 00:04:10,063
Nitrobenzene.

106
00:04:09,924 --> 00:04:11,312
Some keep old names too.

107
00:04:11,312 --> 00:04:12,645
Methylbenzene is toluene.

108
00:04:12,645 --> 00:04:13,977
Hydroxybenzene is phenol.

109
00:04:14,377 --> 00:04:16,220
With two groups, number the ring.

110
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:17,384
One, two is ortho.

111
00:04:17,384 --> 00:04:18,586
One, three is meta.

112
00:04:18,586 --> 00:04:19,749
One, four is para.

113
00:04:19,749 --> 00:04:22,420
So one, four dichlorobenzene
is para dichlorobenzene.

114
00:04:23,070 --> 00:04:24,534
Now, the exam trap.

115
00:04:24,884 --> 00:04:27,996
Students take the chain that
is drawn in a straight line.

116
00:04:27,996 --> 00:04:31,324
A chain with an ethyl on carbon
two becomes two ethyl butane.

117
00:04:31,724 --> 00:04:32,624
Wrong.

118
00:04:32,282 --> 00:04:35,158
The longest chain can
bend into the side group.

119
00:04:35,158 --> 00:04:37,558
Follow it, and you find five carbons.

120
00:04:37,558 --> 00:04:39,912
The real name is three methyl pentane.

121
00:04:40,562 --> 00:04:42,021
Where is this asked?

122
00:04:42,021 --> 00:04:46,359
Boards and entrance tests ask for
the IUPAC name of a given structure,

123
00:04:46,359 --> 00:04:48,631
or for the structure from a name.

124
00:04:49,231 --> 00:04:50,421
Exam level now.

125
00:04:50,421 --> 00:04:53,535
What is the correct IUPAC
name of this ketone?

126
00:04:53,535 --> 00:04:54,775
Pause and solve.

127
00:05:00,175 --> 00:05:01,404
The answer is B.

128
00:05:01,404 --> 00:05:03,213
Four methyl pentan two one.

129
00:05:03,713 --> 00:05:07,786
The longest chain holding the
ketone carbon has five carbons.

130
00:05:07,786 --> 00:05:08,686
Pent.

131
00:05:08,871 --> 00:05:11,422
Number from the end nearer the CO.

132
00:05:11,422 --> 00:05:15,661
The ketone sits on carbon two,
and the methyl on carbon four.

133
00:05:16,161 --> 00:05:18,086
A numbers from the wrong end.

134
00:05:18,086 --> 00:05:19,675
C calls it an aldehyde.

135
00:05:19,675 --> 00:05:21,946
D picks a chain that is too short.

136
00:05:22,546 --> 00:05:24,086
Here is your recap.

137
00:05:24,086 --> 00:05:25,668
Screenshot this card.

138
00:05:28,868 --> 00:05:31,054
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

139
00:05:31,454 --> 00:05:32,354
One.

140
00:05:31,839 --> 00:05:33,784
What does the word root but tell you?

141
00:05:34,584 --> 00:05:35,484
Two.

142
00:05:35,027 --> 00:05:37,026
Name CH three CH two CHO.

143
00:05:37,826 --> 00:05:38,726
Three.

144
00:05:38,277 --> 00:05:40,225
What does para mean in a benzene name?

145
00:05:41,125 --> 00:05:45,853
Next time: how can two molecules have
the very same formula, yet be different?

146
00:05:45,853 --> 00:05:47,493
We find out in isomerism.
